#!/usr/bin/python3
# SPDX-License-Identifier: MIT
"""Bare NetworkManager VPN service plugin for the nm-sshuttle spike.

Implements org.freedesktop.NetworkManager.VPN.Plugin directly with Gio,
starts sshuttle in a systemd unit and reports the result to NetworkManager.
It exists to answer the questions in docs/design.md section 6, not to be the
real plugin: no reconnect policy, no guard table, no health checks beyond
"is the tunnel unit still active".

Rules learned from the Fedora 44 runs (docs/design.md §2.1):
- every GLib callback and D-Bus handler is guarded: an exception becomes
  Failure + StateChanged(STOPPED), never silence (NetworkManager only acts on
  STOPPED);
- Config is sent only after NetworkManager has a device for the tundev, and
  the tundev is never replaced while the VPN is active, except in the
  deliberately risky "relink-wait" test mode;
- the plugin tears down when NetworkManager's bus name vanishes.

Debug hook: SIGUSR1 simulates a dropped tunnel. The profile key
"spike-reconnect" picks how the plugin tells NetworkManager:
  identical    STARTING, then the same Config + Ip4Config + STARTED
               (NetworkManager >= 1.36 never completes this)
  nbns         as identical, but one extra Ip4Config with a sentinel WINS
               entry before the real one, so NetworkManager commits
  nbns2        the sentinel Ip4Config alone, then the real one once
               NetworkManager shows the connection as activated
  perturb      STARTING, then a config whose address alternates between
               192.0.0.8 and 192.0.0.9
  invisible    nothing: NetworkManager keeps showing the VPN as connected
  relink-wait  STARTING, recreate nmss0, wait for NetworkManager's device,
               then Config + Ip4Config (crashed NetworkManager 1.56.1 when
               done without the wait)
  giveup       STARTING, then instead of reconnecting: Failure, remove nmss0,
               STOPPED (the order of runs 1-3)
  giveup-stopped  ... Failure, STOPPED, then remove nmss0 once NetworkManager
               has deactivated the connection (or after 2 s)
  giveup-config   ... re-send the unchanged Config first (NetworkManager
               leaves "connect" for ip-config-get), then as giveup-stopped
  giveup-linklost ... delete nmss0 halfway through the gap, then give up as
               giveup-config does (design §4.4, branch 4)

SIGTERM during a gap gives up as giveup-config does before exiting.

The profile key "spike-idle-quit" (seconds, default 10) sets how long a
stopped plugin waits before it exits. "spike-link-delay" (seconds, default
0.1) is how long a give-up keeps nmss0 after NetworkManager has deactivated
the connection, so that a test can read resolved while the link exists.
"""

